---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ChildGrowthPercentile from '@/components/tools/reference/ChildGrowthPercentile.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="child-growth-percentile-calculator">
  <ChildGrowthPercentile client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the child growth percentile calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The child growth percentile calculator compares a measurement with the 2000 CDC growth charts for
      the same sex and age. Each chart gives three numbers per half month of age: L (skewness), M (the
      median) and S (the spread). The z-score of a measurement x is
      <code>z = ((x / M)^L − 1) / (L × S)</code>, and the percentile is the share of the standard normal
      distribution below z.
    </p>
    <p>
      Under 24 months the calculator uses the infant charts (weight, length lying down and head
      circumference); from 2 to 20 years it uses weight, stature (standing height) and BMI for age.
      The percentile curves on the chart are drawn from the same L, M and S values.
    </p>
    <h2>Reading percentiles</h2>
    <p>
      A child on the 40th percentile for height is taller than 40 of 100 children of the same age and
      sex in the reference population. Healthy children are found on every line; doctors watch whether a
      child stays near the same curve over time. For BMI, the CDC uses the 85th percentile for overweight
      and the 95th for obesity. Very high BMI values above the 97th percentile are less precise with the
      2000 charts.
    </p>
    <h2>CDC and WHO charts</h2>
    <p>
      In the US, the CDC recommends the WHO growth standards for children under 2 and the CDC charts
      from 2 years. This tool uses the CDC charts at all ages, because they are in the public domain;
      results under 2 years can differ a little from the WHO charts that a doctor uses.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
